#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 13pt, weight: "bold")[Acknowledgement]]
#note[Shout-out to Elder Jimmy Larson for this idea and letting me take some things from his own personal dictionary. I couldn\'t have done it without him, nor do I take credit for the entirety of this document. I have added to it as I have seen fit. This document consists of a few things. A dictionary, tips and tricks, grammar principles and rules, tongue placement for accent, etc. There\'s a ton of stuff and I can\'t write it all down. You get the picture. I don\'t know if I will ever even show this to anyone. This is more just for me and to help me gauge my learning ability. But if someone does happen to see this, Uživajte! I started this during my second transfer and added words as I stumbled along them. I couldn\'t put all the words I know in here, but here\'s the majority of them. This also includes the glossary from the Red Book, which my MTC group and I started to memorize in my second transfer. Anyways, I hope this helps. Keep going strong till the end and hopefully this makes language study a little more interesting. Until we see each other again, Keep living the dream!

— Elder Levi Mackay \(July 2022–July 2024\)
]
#strong[FYI:] This dictionary includes many of the swear words from the Serbian swear words book I have. I have censored them a little, but read at your own risk.

#strong[FYI pt. 2:] I couldn\'t put everything in here. I apologize. As I got later into the mission, I didn\'t have as much time to go back and edit this, so all of the labeling isn\'t entirely complete. If something doesn\'t make sense, or you have any questions, please feel free to shoot me a text or a voice recording and ask about it. I always love answering language questions and I\'ll still be available to help when I\'m at home.

#v(0.3em) #line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.4pt + luma(200)) #v(0.3em)
#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 13pt, weight: "bold")[A Note Before You Start]]
So here\'s the thing — this started as a working document. I was adding to it clear up until my last stretch in the field, and I never really got the chance to sit down and finish it the way I wanted to. Mission life doesn\'t slow down for that kind of thing, yk? A lot of it got built transfer by transfer, word by word, whenever I stumbled onto something worth writing down.

After I got home, some of the gaps got closed — entries filled in, sequences finished out, stress marks added where I\'d left blanks. Anywhere you see a #strong[#degmark ] next to something, that\'s one of those spots. It means I didn\'t personally verify it while I was still there, so treat it as a solid guess rather than something I\'d stake my name on. Everything without a #degmark  is stuff I checked myself in the field, with real people, in real conversations — trust that first, always.

If a #degmark  entry looks off to you, it\'s worth double-checking with a native before you build your whole vocabulary around it. This is still fundamentally the same document I was building the whole time — just finished. Uživajte.

